WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
Strong TypeScript types reduce the number of unsafe states the program has to defend against at runtime.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.760
They do not replace validation at trust boundaries, but they make validated data easier to use correctly.

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:19.100
A defensive codebase uses types to document intent, constrain behavior, and make maintenance mistakes visible early.

4
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.020
At boundaries, prefer unknown over any.

5
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.840
The any type lets a value flow through the program as if every operation is allowed.

6
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.100
It suppresses the compiler at the exact point where you need the most discipline.

7
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:38.880
The unknown type is safer because it forces deliberate narrowing before the value can be read, called, indexed, or passed into a narrower contract.

8
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.360
Strict compiler settings are also defensive.

9
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.940
Strict null checks make the difference between missing and present values visible.

10
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.360
No implicit any prevents untyped values from quietly appearing in the codebase.

11
00:00:51.460 --> 00:01:00.900
Not every legacy project can enable every strict option at once, but teams can move toward stricter settings module by module and treat compiler feedback as design information.

12
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.020
Model valid states directly.

13
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.520
Discriminated unions often express workflow states better than loose flags and many optional fields.

14
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:17.060
For example, a request can be loading, failed, or ready, with each case carrying only the fields that belong to that case.

15
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:22.680
This reduces combinations that should never exist and makes impossible states harder to represent.

16
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:29.200
Readonly data, narrow function contracts, and explicit return types also support defensive design.

17
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:37.060
They reduce accidental mutation, make ownership clearer, and help reviewers see what a function promises to accept and return.

18
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:44.600
Exhaustive handling is especially useful when a union gains a new case, because the compiler can point to code that must be revisited.

19
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.920
The point is not to create clever types that only one person understands.

20
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:57.460
The point is to move important assumptions into a form the compiler, reviewers, and future maintainers can see.

21
00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:03.720
Strong types are defensive when they make correct use easier, incorrect use harder, and runtime checks more focused.
